ent hand. Since I have a pair of F2s, maybe keeping them is better. The other tiles are in different suits. Discarding W1 or T1 might be the way to go. Let's check the valid actions: Play W1 is an option. If I discard W1, then my hand has T1, which is a terminal. Maybe that helps with All Terminals and Honors if I can get rid of the other non-terminal tiles, but that seems unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W5, which is a middle tile not part of any chow. But then I lose a tile that could be part of a chow if I had W4 and W6. Not helpful.\n\nAnother angle: the user's melds are empty, so they're trying to build a concealed hand. Concealed hands can get Fully Concealed Hand (4 points). If they discard a tile and keep all others concealed, that's good. But the valid actions include Play B5, etc. So the user is discarding a tile to keep the rest concealed.\n\nIf I discard a tile that's not part of any meld, like W1, then I can keep the rest concealed. The same for T1. Let's say I discard W1. Then my hand has T1, T4, T5, etc. Maybe that's better.\n\nBut considering the unshown tiles, maybe some tiles are more likely to be drawn. For example, if I discard B5, there are 3 left. If I draw one, I can form a pung. But since I'm discarding now, it's a trade-off.\n\nIn the end, maybe the safest and most strategic move is to discard a tile that's not part of any potential meld in my hand. W1 is a terminal not part of any chow. So Play W1.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The hand contains a pair of South Winds (F2) and several Dots (B2, B4, B5, B7, B8, B9). Discarding W1 (East Wind) removes a non-contributing terminal, preserving potential for concealed hand and possible patterns like All Terminals and Honors if other non-terminals are discarded later.\n\nReason: W1 is a terminal not part of any chow or pung in the current hand. Discarding it maintains flexibility for concealed hand patterns and avoids exposing a tile that could be claimed by others for chows. The pair of F2 (South Wind) aligns with the prevailing wind, potentially aiding future scoring.\n\nAnswer: Play W1"}
